  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nA low pressure system will bring isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms and windy conditions to the area today. These \nshowers and thunderstorms are currently, as of 1:30PM MDT, moving \neast through south-central Montana. Expect these showers and \nthunderstorms to move through eastern Montana through the \nafternoon before coming to an end this evening. The main risk with\nthis activity is gusty and erratic winds, brief heavy rain, and \nhail. Cannot rule out a few strong to severe thunderstorms today, \nand have already seen a few severe wind gusts over central \nMontana. Outside of this activity, the main concern today is the \nwidespread windy conditions that are developing across the area. \nHave already seen wind gusts of 25 to 50 mph (outside of a few \nhigher convective wind gusts), strongest over the foothills west \nof Billings. Look for these winds to continue into this evening, \ngradually spreading east through the day. While winds will subside\ntonight, breezy conditions will continue overnight generally \naround and north of a Livingston to Billings to Ekalaka line. \n\nTuesday will be another windy day as the low pressure system \nlifts to the northeast. With this, west to northwesterly wind \ngusts of 20 to 45 mph are forecast, strongest over eastern \nMontana. While mostly dry conditions will prevail, a shortwave \ntrough behind the low pressure system may bring enough lift to \nsupport isolated showers over south-central Montana during the \nday. With that said, the downsloping winds may limit some of this \nactivity to virga. Either way, locally enhanced winds are possible\nunder showers or virga on Tuesday. \n\nWith the wind today and Tuesday, elevated fire weather conditions\nexist. Take care to not cause a spark! \n\nWednesday into Thursday look to be dry days as temperatures warm \nback into the 80s after a cooler day in the 70s on Tuesday. During\nthis time, the combination of breezy winds, low relative \nhumidity, and warm temperatures will lead to localized elevated \nfire weather conditions, especially on Thursday. \n\nFriday into the weekend, the forecast becomes more uncertain. \nHowever, a weather system looks to impact the area in some fashion\nduring this time, bringing cooler temperatures and the chance of \nprecipitation back to the area. With this, temperatures may be \ncool enough for some early season snow in the mountains. Stay up \nto date with the forecast though the week, especially if you have \nplans in the mountains next weekend. LA",
